            <ArticleTitle>Incidental discovery of two levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine systems misplaced in the peritoneal cavity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>441-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/13625187.2010.514081</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a rare case of the incidental discovery of two misplaced intrauterine devices in the same patient, and to discuss risk factors for uterine perforation and appropriate management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 37-year-old woman complaining of chronic lower back pain presented with an X-ray of her abdomen showing two misplaced LNG-IUSs in the peritoneal cavity. More than two years before the patient had submitted within two months to two successive attempts at insertion of a Mirena® IUS. The threads of the first IUS were not seen at the follow-up visit and the IUS was not detected on ultrasound either. As it was assumed to have been expelled, insertion of a second IUS of the same type was attempted. Again, the threads were not seen at the control visit. This time, no further action was undertaken.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Radiography is helpful in confirming migration of an intrauterine contraceptive. Removal of the latter is recommended, due to the potential for serious complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
